;;; A relation parameter its activator binds to a nested fact reads nothing,
;;; in silence — **W24**, regression `nested-fact-activator`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule `r` whose parameter `?R` names the relation of its inner pattern,
;;; `(box (?R b))`, and the activator `(r (a b))`, which binds `?R` to the
;;; nested fact `(a b)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A relation is named by a symbol, and a nested fact binds no parameter.
;;; An activator binding a relation parameter to a nested fact is refused at
;;; load where it is written (Q-M1e2.18), and so is a rule that could derive
;;; one through a variable (Q-M1f.1). Every command that loads this program
;;; exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; At `f8c10730` (`ein saturate --dump FILE` and `ein solve FILE`, release
;;; and debug) the program loaded, and the inner pattern was an opaque slot
;;; (the parameter unbound), which matched nothing, so `r` never fired: root
;;; held no `hit`, exit 0, in silence; the same pattern at the top level,
;;; `(?R b)`, stopped compilation with a `CompileError` in the Python IR's
;;; spelling. The loader refuses it: *kb
;;; load error: rule 'r': its parameter ?R names a relation, and (r (a b))
;;; binds it to the fact (a b) — a relation is named by a symbol, not a
;;; fact*.
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation box Node :why "{?1} is in a box") (relation c Node :why "c holds of {?1}") (relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit") (relation a Node :why "a holds of {?1}") (relation rel Node :why "rel holds of {?1}") (relation item Node :why "{?1} is an item")
(box (a b)) (box (c (a b))) (not (a b)) (rel a) (item i)
(rule r (?R) :match (box (?R b)) :assert (hit z) :why "({?R} b) is in a box, so z is hit")
(r (a b))
